# WHAT IT ADDS: a VERB-FRAME SLOT-OCCUPANCY factor to the Competition-Model coarse role labeler.
# Today coarse_roles labels every nominal by an INDEPENDENT per-token argmax, so a verb can be given
# two OBJECTs (a bare temporal/measure nominal after the object scores OBJ; "She saw him yesterday"
# -> him:iobj, yesterday:obj, live 2026-09-13). The brain assigns a verb's arguments JOINTLY: the
# frame licenses a fixed slot inventory (subject/object/[recipient if ditransitive]/by-agent), each
# slot holds ONE filler (cue-based retrieval, Lewis & Vasishth 2005; a filled slot lowers its
# availability for a second same-type filler), and a displaced core filler falls to an oblique/adjunct.
#
# KNOWLEDGE IN COUNTS (plastic, online-observable): lambda[slot] = -log P(a 2nd filler of slot s | the
# verb already has one), counted per verb token from reading (UD-EWT train here). The counts confirm
# capacity-one directly: a verb takes 2 objects 0/9516, 2 recipients 0/647, 2 by-agents 0/306, 2
# subjects 84/13048 (coordination). The recipient (iobj) slot exists only for verbs whose learned
# frame takes an indirect object (lemma_frames, already in the asset) -- the frame defines the inventory.
#
